'''Aplysia''' (ap-lis′i-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL.; cf. L. ''aplysiæ'', pl., < Gr. ἀπλυσίαι, pl., prop. gen. sing., ἀπλυσίας σπόγγος, a sponge, so named from its dirty-gray color, < Gr. ἀπλυσία, filthiness, < ἄπλυτος, unwashed, < ἀ- priv. + πλυτός, verbal
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Depilatory Sea-hare (''Aplysia depilans'').}}}}
adj. of πλύνειν, wash.] A genus of gastropodous mollusks, the sea-hares, having an oval oblong form with four tentacles, and somewhat resembling slugs. {{smaller|Its numerous species are remarkable for the function of secreting a fluid of violet color (due to the presence of iodine), which they discharge when molested. One of the best known is ''A. depilans'', the depilatory sea-hare, so called because it was supposed that the fluid it discharged was capable of removing hair or preventing its growth. Also written ''Laplysia'', by an original mistake (Linnæus, 1767), followed by many writers.}}

'''aplysiid''' (ap-lis′i-id), ''n.'' A gastropod of the family ''Aplysiidæ''.

'''Aplysiidæ''' (ap-li-sī′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Aplysia'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of tectibranchiate gastropods, of which the genus ''Aplysia'' is the type, having the shell rudimentary or wanting. {{smaller|Besides ''Aplysia'', there are several other genera, as ''Dolabella'' and ''Notarchus'', and the species are numerous. Also incorrectly written ''Aplysiadæ'' and ''Aplysidæ''. The same group is also named ''Aplysiacea'', ''Aplysiana'', and ''Aplysianæ''.}}

'''apneumatic''' (ap-nū-mat′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀπνεύματος, not blown through (< ἀ- priv. + πνεῦμα(τ-), breath, blowing), + ''-ic'': see ''a-''<sup>18</sup> and

