'''apoblast''' (ap′ō-blast), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀπό, off, + βλαστός, germ.] In ''biol.'', a so-called directive corpuscle; a small temporary body formed in an unimpregnated ovum as a result of cell-division. See extract.

{{smaller block|Resting on the dividing upper sphere are the eight-shaped “directive corpuscles,” better called “præseminal outcast cells or ''apoblasts'',” since they are the result of a cell-division which affects the egg-cell before it is impregnated, and are mere refuse destined to disappear.
{{right|''E. R. Lankester'', Encyc. Brit., XVI. 637.}}}}

